(version 1)

# Custom design rules for a board made and assembled at JLCPCB: a TEMPLATE.
#
# How to use it
#   1. Copy this file into the KiCad project folder as <board name>.kicad_dru (the same base name as the
#      .kicad_pcb), BEFORE the first zone fill and the first routing.
#   2. Set every value under a line marked SET from JLCPCB's live capability page for THIS layer count and
#      copper weight, on the day, and write the date here:
#          values read on: <date>, page: <name>
#      The numbers below are common values with margin above the minimum; they are a starting point, not
#      a quote of the supplier.
#   3. Replace the EXAMPLE rules at the end by the board's own net classes, one rule per class.
#   4. Run `python -I scripts/drc_run.py`. It proves that this file was loaded. KiCad ignores the WHOLE file
#      without a word when one rule has a syntax error, and then reports a clean board.
#   5. Prove each rule once on a copy that contains the defect (a too thin track, a via on a pad). A rule whose
#      condition is mistyped never fires, and no tool reports that.
#
# Syntax traps of this file format
#   - A comment must stand on a line of its own. A "#" after a rule on the same line is a syntax error, and
#     one syntax error switches off every rule of the file, silently.
#   - Lengths carry their unit without a blank: 0.15mm.
#   - A (layer ...) line narrows a rule to items ON that layer. A rule between a silkscreen text and a pad, or
#     between a via and a pad, must not have one, or it never fires.
#   - A minimum connection width finds NECKS between wider copper. Copper that is narrow everywhere is not a
#     neck: check track widths with track_width.
#
# Why custom rules and not only "Board Setup": the board-setup minimums are one number for the whole board.
# These rules add what board setup cannot say: per net class, per layer, text against pads, a via hole against
# a pad of any net. Every rule is an ERROR: warnings are read once and then ignored.
#
# Also set in Board Setup > Design Rules > Violation Severity (stored in the .kicad_pro; a rule file cannot
# set them): Error for courtyards overlap, footprint has no courtyard, hole inside a courtyard, thermal relief
# incomplete, copper sliver, silkscreen clipped by solder mask, silkscreen overlap, text height and thickness.
# drc_run.py lists every check that the project has set to "ignore".

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Copper: track width and spacing
# Why: the fabricator's minimum is a limit of its process, not a target. Margin costs nothing on a sample
# board and removes a class of engineering questions.

# SET: minimum track width for this layer count, plus margin
(rule "JLCPCB: minimum track width"
  (severity error)
  (constraint track_width (min 0.15mm)))

# SET: minimum copper spacing for this layer count, plus margin
(rule "JLCPCB: minimum copper spacing"
  (severity error)
  (constraint clearance (min 0.15mm)))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Copper to the board edge
# Why: the outline is routed with a tolerance; copper at the edge is exposed, burred or cut. A zone saved
# before this rule existed keeps its old fill: refill, save, and measure the exported Gerbers too
# (edge_check.py), because DRC judges a refilled copy.

# SET: the page's recommended value, not its minimum
(rule "JLCPCB: copper to board edge"
  (severity error)
  (constraint edge_clearance (min 0.3mm)))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Holes
# Why: a drill below the minimum is refused or priced as a special process; holes too close break out into
# each other; a thin ring breaks out of its pad when the drill wanders.

# SET: smallest plated drill at no extra cost
(rule "JLCPCB: plated hole, smallest drill"
  (severity error)
  (condition "A.isPlated()")
  (constraint hole_size (min 0.3mm)))

# SET: smallest non-plated hole
(rule "JLCPCB: non-plated hole, smallest drill"
  (severity error)
  (condition "A.Type == 'Pad' && !A.isPlated()")
  (constraint hole_size (min 0.5mm)))

# SET: via pad that goes with the drill above at no extra cost
(rule "JLCPCB: via pad diameter"
  (severity error)
  (condition "A.Type == 'Via'")
  (constraint via_diameter (min 0.6mm)))

# SET: (via pad - via drill) / 2 from the page, plus margin
(rule "JLCPCB: via annular ring"
  (severity error)
  (condition "A.Type == 'Via'")
  (constraint annular_width (min 0.13mm)))

# SET: ring of a component hole; hand-soldered pads want more
(rule "JLCPCB: through-hole pad annular ring"
  (severity error)
  (condition "A.Type == 'Pad' && A.isPlated()")
  (constraint annular_width (min 0.2mm)))

# SET: drilled wall to drilled wall
(rule "JLCPCB: hole to hole"
  (severity error)
  (constraint hole_to_hole (min 0.5mm)))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. No via in or against an SMD pad
# Why: an open or tented via in a pad wicks the solder away from the joint; filled and capped via-in-pad is
# a paid option on most stacks. The via HOLE keeps its own ring away from every SMD pad, whatever the net.

# SET: at least the via's annular ring
(rule "via hole to SMD pad"
  (severity error)
  (condition "A.Type == 'Via' && B.Type == 'Pad' && B.Pad_Type == 'SMD'")
  (constraint physical_hole_clearance (min 0.15mm)))

# The one exception: thermal vias inside an exposed thermal pad, as the maker's drawing shows them (drill and
# pitch from the datasheet). Keep the paste windows between the vias, and have them covered on the far side
# where the fabricator offers it. Exempt such a footprint from the rule BY NAME, in two places:
#   1. here: replace the condition line of the rule above by one that names the footprint, for example for the
#      footprints with the references U7 and U9:
#
#        (condition "A.Type == 'Via' && B.Type == 'Pad' && B.Pad_Type == 'SMD' && !B.memberOfFootprint('U7') && !B.memberOfFootprint('U9')")
#
#   2. in board.toml, with the reason, so that drc_run.py lists the exemption and a reviewer sees it:
#
#        [drc.via_in_pad_exempt]
#        U7 = "exposed pad with the maker's 3 x 3 thermal vias, 0.3 mm drill (datasheet page 31)"
#
# memberOfFootprint takes a reference (U7), a pattern (U1*) or a footprint name. Name single parts: a pattern
# that catches more than the parts with a drawn via pattern switches the rule off where it is needed.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Silkscreen
# Why: ink on a pad is removed by the fabricator or spoils the joint; text below the minimum height or
# stroke fills in and cannot be read; the print is registered to the copper only within a tolerance.

# SET: the page's silkscreen registration tolerance, plus margin
# No (layer ...) line in this rule: with one, KiCad 9 never applies it (the pad is not on a silkscreen layer),
# and nothing reports that. Proven on a board where such a rule had been "active" and never fired.
(rule "silkscreen text to pads"
  (severity error)
  (condition "(A.Type == 'Text' || A.Type == 'Field') && B.Type == 'Pad'")
  (constraint silk_clearance (min 0.2mm)))

# SET: smallest legible text height of the page; 1.0 mm is the floor, never go below it
(rule "silkscreen text height"
  (severity error)
  (layer "?.Silkscreen")
  (condition "A.Type == 'Text' || A.Type == 'Field'")
  (constraint text_height (min 1.0mm)))

# SET: smallest silkscreen line width of the page, plus margin. 0.15 mm was the published minimum when this
# file was written (as read in 2026-11): re-read the live minimum. The value here is a little above it on
# purpose.
(rule "silkscreen text stroke"
  (severity error)
  (layer "?.Silkscreen")
  (condition "A.Type == 'Text' || A.Type == 'Field'")
  (constraint text_thickness (min 0.16mm)))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. Courtyards
# Why: two bodies in one place cannot be assembled; the pick-and-place nozzle and the rework iron need the
# room the courtyard reserves. A footprint without a courtyard is invisible to this rule: make "footprint
# has no courtyard" an error in the violation severities.

# SET: 0 = courtyards may touch; give a positive value for hand-assembled boards
(rule "courtyards do not overlap"
  (severity error)
  (constraint courtyard_clearance (min 0.0mm)))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. Thermal reliefs
# Why: a pad that reaches its zone through one spoke carries the pad's whole current through that spoke
# and can be a dry joint after reflow. Power pads usually want a solid connection instead (set it on the
# pad or the zone), and then this rule does not apply to them.

# SET: 2 as a floor; 4 where a pad carries current
(rule "thermal relief: at least two spokes"
  (severity error)
  (constraint min_resolved_spokes 2))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. EXAMPLES: minimum widths per net class. Replace by the board's own classes, one block per class.
# Why: a net class is a setting for new tracks, not a proof about the tracks that exist. These rules make a
# track or a zone neck below the class width an error. The width comes from the current worksheet
# (current, copper thickness, allowed temperature rise and voltage drop), not from habit. A rule for a
# class that the board does not have never fires and does no harm, but delete it so the file says what
# the board needs.

# SET: the class name and the width from the current worksheet
(rule "EXAMPLE power class: track width"
  (severity error)
  (condition "A.NetClass == 'POWER'")
  (constraint track_width (min 0.5mm)))

# SET: one rule per copper layer that carries this class; the width from the current worksheet
(rule "EXAMPLE power class: narrowest zone neck, top layer"
  (severity error)
  (layer "F.Cu")
  (condition "A.NetClass == 'POWER' && A.Type == 'Zone'")
  (constraint connection_width (min 0.5mm)))

# SET: a net that needs its own width
(rule "EXAMPLE single net: track width"
  (severity error)
  (condition "A.NetName == '/VBUS' || A.NetName == 'VBUS'")
  (constraint track_width (min 0.8mm)))
